# HTML Reading UI Rule

Human-facing knowledge artifacts in this repo are HTML-first.

Canonical data still lives in Markdown (`research/sources/`, `research/intake/`,
`research/questions/`, `research/notes/`), but the user should read HTML first.

Follow [docs/HTML_READING_UI_GUIDE.md](mdc:docs/HTML_READING_UI_GUIDE.md).

## Required Behavior

- If a user is expected to read, learn, review, or revisit an artifact, create or
  update an HTML reading page.
- Prefer HTML links inside HTML pages.
- Markdown links are allowed only as fallback and must be explicitly labeled:
  `md source`, `markdown intake`, or `markdown canonical`.
- Do not label a Markdown link as just `source`, `intake`, or `Q&A`.
- If no specific HTML page exists, link to the closest HTML reading view first.

## Required Page Structure

Every human-facing HTML page should include:

- Breadcrumb.
- Clear H1 title.
- One-paragraph summary / lede.
- Status or source-quality metadata.
- Short, scannable sections.
- Source confidence near claims.
- Follow-up / unknowns when applicable.
- HTML-first links at the bottom.

## Style Constraints

- Static HTML only.
- Inline CSS only.
- No framework.
- No external assets.
- No JavaScript unless a later milestone explicitly requests interaction.
- No gradients, decorative animations, heavy shadows, or emoji icons.
- Keep it readable on mobile widths.

## Review Checklist

Before committing:

- [ ] Can the page be understood without opening Markdown?
- [ ] Are primary / secondary / unverified claims clearly labeled?
- [ ] Are high-risk claims accompanied by plain-language warnings?
- [ ] Are Markdown links explicitly fallback links?
- [ ] Is the page useful for re-reading, not just visually nicer?
